1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:10,088
Picture two fields growing the same crop, one farmed organically and one
not. At harvest, does the organic field come up short, and by how much?

2
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:18,725
Ask around and you hear both answers with total confidence:
organic grows less, or organic grows just as much.

3
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:25,171
And the answer sets up a second question: if organic
does grow less, is it still the greener choice?

4
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:34,200
AsapSCIENCE told its viewers that "organic crop yields
are 25% lower on average than conventional ones".

5
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:41,563
Mark Hyman says the opposite: "you can grow as much food
or more food better food without all those chemicals".

6
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:43,687
So who is right?

7
00:00:44,200 --> 00:01:05,813
A meta-analysis by Lauren Ponisio and colleagues pooled side-by-side yield comparisons and found organic yields 19.2% lower than conventional, with a likely
range of 15.5 to 22.9%, and the authors warn that even this may overstate the gap, because the published studies lean towards reporting bigger conventional wins.

8
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:14,408
Conventional came out ahead for every crop type the study grouped,
and for most of those types the gap was about the same size.

9
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:20,196
That gap is real, but it is smaller than the
ones earlier meta-analyses had reported.

10
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:34,325
Why lower at all? A review of organic food and health, citing earlier work, points to fertiliser: in organic systems
crop yield is typically limited by how much nitrogen the plants can get, which is not the case in conventional systems.

11
00:01:34,333 --> 00:01:43,958
Not everyone agrees: a clinical report for pediatricians says well-designed
farming studies show organic yields comparable to conventional farming.

12
00:01:44,267 --> 00:01:51,629
But in the report's summary, the part we could read, that
is one sentence, with no yield figure and no studies named.

13
00:01:52,633 --> 00:02:04,808
So on the pooled data, yes: organic fields grew less, by roughly a fifth or perhaps a little
less, but where crops were rotated or grown in mixtures the gap shrank to about 8 or 9%.

14
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:15,271
Which raises the next question: if each organic field grows less, does organic
need more land for the same food, and does that undo its green reputation?

15
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:21,563
Harvard Health put it plainly: "Organic
foods are clearly healthier for the planet".

16
00:02:21,567 --> 00:02:30,567
The same pediatric report agrees, saying organic farming "has been
demonstrated to have less environmental impact than conventional approaches".

17
00:02:30,567 --> 00:02:41,029
But a meta-analysis by Clark and Tilman measured the impacts per unit of food, not
per field, using studies that assessed both systems within the same publication.

18
00:02:41,533 --> 00:02:47,933
Measured that way, organic systems
needed 25 to 110% more land.

19
00:02:48,233 --> 00:02:56,796
They used 15% less energy, but had 37% higher
eutrophication potential, a measure of nutrient pollution.

20
00:02:57,100 --> 00:03:06,487
Greenhouse gas emissions came out similar, because relying on manure turns
more of a farm's nitrogen into nitrous oxide, a potent greenhouse gas.

21
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:14,688
The authors add that these are comparisons at a local scale, and
the picture might differ regionally, nationally or globally.

22
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:27,987
And they warn against reading this as a win for conventional farming, which uses more energy and
relies on high fertiliser, herbicide and pesticide inputs that can harm health and the environment.

23
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:41,613
What does dwarf the organic question is the food itself: on every indicator they examined, ruminant meats had
20 to 100 times the impacts of plant-based foods, a difference far larger than the one between farming systems.

24
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:52,883
So per unit of food, organic is not clearly greener: it trades lower energy
use for more land and more nutrient pollution, with emissions about even.

25
00:03:53,900 --> 00:04:00,813
The next time a label or a video says organic grows
just as much, ask which studies, and how many.

26
00:04:00,833 --> 00:04:08,533
And when something is sold as better for the planet, ask:
better per what? Per plate of food, the answer here was mixed.
